          "question": "I am 68 with COPD and I have been more breathless for three days with green sputum. Is this an emergency, and what does guidance describe about exacerbations?",
          "answer": "The automated system did not find an emergency pattern in its red-flag table for your description, though this does not mean your symptoms are not serious. If your symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, you should seek medical advice (diagnostic specialist). Urgent assessment is advised for several symptoms (condition-copd, guideline-copd-diagnosis-and-stepwise-management, diagnostic specialist). These include worsening breathlessness that does not respond to the usual reliever, breathlessness at rest, confusion or unusual drowsiness, blue discoloration of the lips or fingers, chest pain, coughing blood, or a fever with a marked change in sputum (condition-copd, guideline-copd-diagnosis-and-stepwise-management, diagnostic specialist). You mentioned increased breathlessness and green sputum (diagnostic specialist).\n\nA COPD exacerbation is defined as an acute worsening of your usual respiratory symptoms that goes beyond your normal day-to-day variations (condition-copd, research specialist). These are typically brought on by a viral or bacterial infection (condition-copd, research specialist). Each exacerbation can worsen your quality of life, speed up the decline in your lung function, and increase the likelihood of future exacerbations (condition-copd, research specialist). Recovering to your baseline health can take weeks, and some people may not fully return to their previous level of function (condition-copd, research specialist). A history of exacerbations is also the strongest predictor of future ones (condition-copd, research specialist).\n\nGeneral management for COPD includes smoking cessation, inhaled bronchodilator therapy (with inhaled corticosteroids if you have exacerbations or a high eosinophil count), pulmonary rehabilitation, and vaccination against influenza, pneumococcal disease, COVID-19, and RSV (condition-copd, guideline-copd-diagnosis-and-stepwise-management, research specialist). Assessment for long-term oxygen therapy is also part of management if your resting oxygen levels are severely low, as this can affect survival (condition-copd, guideline-copd-diagnosis-and-stepwise-management, research specialist). It is recommended to have a written plan for what to do during an exacerbation (condition-copd, research specialist). Your treatment plan should be reviewed at least once a year, after every exacerbation, and after any changes in your inhaled therapy, with your inhaler technique checked at each review (guideline-copd-diagnosis-and-stepwise-management, research specialist).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
